The Arrays.compare() method in Java compares two arrays lexicographically
Negative integer (< 0): The first array is lexicographically smaller than the second.Positive integer (> 0): The first array is lexicographically larger than the second.
returns 0 if both are completely identical
int[] arrayX = {9, 9, 1, 2, 3};
int[] arrayY = {1, 2, 3, 8, 8};
// Compare sub-range {1, 2, 3} of both arrays
int result = Arrays.compare(arrayX, 2, 5, arrayY, 0, 3); 
// Returns 0 (both subsets are completely identical)

Arrays.mismatch(a, aFromIndex, aToIndex, b, bFromIndex, bToIndex);
The Arrays.mismatch(a, b) method in Java compares two arrays element by element. It returns the index of the first position where the arrays differ, or -1 if the arrays are identical in length and elements.If one array is a proper prefix of the other (e.g., [1, 2] and [1, 2, 3]), it returns the length of the smaller array. It is generally much faster than writing custom iteration loops.


Copying Arrays: 

for(int i=0;i<arr.length;i++){
    copy[i]=arr[i];
}

int[] copy = arr.clone();
System.arraycopy(arr,0,copy,0,arr.length);
public static void arraycopy(Object src, int srcPos, Object dest, int destPos, int length)
src − This is the source array.

srcPos − This is the starting position in the source array.

dest − This is the destination array.

destPos − This is the starting position in the destination data.

length − This is the number of array elements to be copied.

